    "body": "EICMA 2026 • Triumph TF-E Bikes Get Even More Enduro With Updates for the 2027 Model Year\nSuzuki Recalls Toyota RAV4 PHEV-Twinned 2026 Across Over Hybrid Control Software Issue\n7 photos\nPhoto: Suzuki\nPreviously used to designate a 250-cc motorcycle, the Across nameplate returned in 2020 for a plug-in hybrid crossover that isn't actually produced by Suzuki. Essentially a rebadged Toyota RAV4 PHEV with minor stylistic changes, the Across has been recalled for motor-generator control electronics that may fail to initialize as intended.\nA failed initialization forces the compact-sized model into limp mode, and in certain cases, it may also cut electric propulsion altogether at high speeds. Toyota has already launched a substantially larger safety recall for the RAV4 PHEV in the United States market, where documents published by the NHTSA offer a lot more detail than Germany's KBA does.\nPer the Kraftfahrt-Bundesamt, which is Germany's Federal Motor Transport Authority, an estimated 1,218 vehicles have to be updated across Europe. The Japanese automaker moved 199 such vehicles in Germany alone. Referred to as 99XW by Suzuki, the campaign covers 2026 model year Across crossovers produced between March 30, 2026 and September 2, 2026.\nJust like the RAV4 with plug-in hybrid assistance, the PHEV-only Across relies on a 2.5-liter naturally aspirated inline-four developed and produced by Toyota. Together with the plug-in side of the powertrain, the system generates in excess of 300 horsepower at full chatter. Its high-voltage battery, meanwhile, is rated at 22.7 kilowatt-hours.\nInstead of mechanical all-wheel drive, the Across further uses the e-rear axle of the RAV4 PHEV. The larger of the two Japanese automakers calls it Electronic On-Demand AWD because the rear-mounted electric drive unit does not operate at all times. Instead, the motor in question supplies instant torque when the vehicle detects a loss of traction at the front or during hard acceleration.\nPhoto: Suzuki Most of the time, the RAV4 PHEV and the Across operate in FWD mode to save fuel. In Europe, the Across is exclusively offered with electrified all-wheel drive for 2026. As of this writing, the Across is the most expensive Suzuki automobile in Germany. Slotted well above the e Vitara and the internal combustion Vitara, the Across is advertised from 55,490 euros at the very least.\nA 4,500-euro electrified vehicle subsidy is advertised as well, along with four paint colors, and a weighted combined energy consumption of 13.0 kWh/100 km plus 1.4 liters of dinosaur juice per 100 kilometers. Without any juice in the high-voltage battery, the combined fuel consumption is 5.4 l/100 km (43.6 mpg US).\nGiven the complexity of the Across' powertrain, the combustion engine and the electric motors have to coordinate their output as the vehicle moves between different operating conditions. The control system, therefore, must initialize the motor-generator units correctly. G92A0-42460 is the part number of the motor-generator electronic control unit.\nIntegrated into the inverter assembly, this ECU manages the electric motor-generator units. Toyota became aware of this problem when a Japanese-market RAV4 PHEV displayed warning lights and subsequently reverted to front-wheel-drive operation in limp mode.\nFor 2026 Suzuki Across owners, authorized service centers must reprogram the motor-generator control unit. Of course, at no charge whatsoever.\nVia // KBA\nLiked the article? Follow us to see more stories from autoevolution in your Google feeds.\n2026 Suzuki Across Suzuki Across Suzuki PHEV recall\nAbout the author: Mircea Panait\nAfter a 1:43 scale model of a Ferrari 250 GTO sparked Mircea's interest for cars when he was a kid, an early internship at Top Gear sealed his career path. He's most interested in muscle cars and American trucks, but he takes a passing interest in quirky kei cars as well. Full profile\nUP NEXT This 2023 BMW M5 Competition Is THE Perfect Antidote to the G90, V8 Sedan Was Over $125K New",
